WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Clone the synthetic shelf and run a one-change temperature case.
Why: Keep the source inputs, inspect native fields, and save a complete result package.
Look for: Prepare run.py with only T = 254.15 K instead of 255.15 K.

2
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Start here in CryoLauncher. Choose Cloud for this tutorial.
Why: Your workspace keeps scripts, dataset references and results together.
Look for: Your workspace keeps scripts, datasets, run history and results together.

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Choose Cloud → Advanced → Icepack → Tutorial / 02 Synthetic Ice Shelf.
Why: Choose the intended interface, model and example before editing or launching a run.
Look for: Use an owned workspace clone before replacing source.

4
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Enter a new name and choose Clone to My Workspace.
Why: Cloning gives you an editable copy while preserving the supplied application source.
Look for: The canonical application source stays read-only.

5
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Wait for the cloned-workspace confirmation and saved file status.
Why: Cloning gives you an editable copy while preserving the supplied application source.
Look for: This owned example is pilot-icepack-advanced-1118.

6
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Choose the one-change run.py in your file picker; Open.
Why: Selecting local files prepares them for import; importing saves them in this example.
Look for: Check that one script file is selected.

7
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Check the selected script before replacing the existing file.
Why: Selecting local files prepares them for import; importing saves them in this example.
Look for: Retain both 400-year phases and 200 steps per phase: dt = 2 years.

8
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Enable Replace existing script files.
Why: Replacement explicitly allows an imported file to overwrite the same name in this clone.
Look for: Your clone already contains run.py.

9
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Choose Import script files.
Why: Import saves the selected source files in this example; it does not launch the model.
Look for: Importing saves the replacement; it does not launch a model.

10
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Wait for Imported run.py. Files are saved.
Why: Import saves the selected source files in this example; it does not launch the model.
Look for: Keep run.py as the Run target.

11
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Check cloud readiness and retain 2 vCPU / 8 GiB.
Why: Ready indicates that the required account resources are configured for the run.
Look for: Review the owned clone and run.py target.

12
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:13.000
Choose Review & Launch.
Why: Review confirms the intended model, source, resources and charges before submission.
Look for: The displayed runtime is an estimate, not measured model duration.

13
00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Control guide: confirm Launch cloud run when checks are Ready.
Why: This confirmation stages the reviewed inputs and starts a paid cloud job.
Look for: The following card belongs to this tutorial’s original native run.

14
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Follow the new Advanced clone run card.
Why: Submission, queue/startup and native model execution are separate stages.
Look for: Wait for completion, then open View results.

15
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Select icepack → velocity in Results; Render when needed.
Why: The solution, field name, units and mesh counts identify what you are viewing.
Look for: Read the field units and selected solution.

16
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Check the native field metadata.
Why: The solution, field name, units and mesh counts identify what you are viewing.
Look for: 3,371 vertices / 6,533 triangles; native CG2 fields exported as portable CG1.

17
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Inspect the complete native velocity map.
Why: Keep the source, versions, hashes and scientific qualifications with the result.
Look for: Speed: 160.457236–728.806914 m/year; arrows show direction.

18
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Read the recorded thickness and damage checks.
Why: These checks complement the velocity map shown here.
Look for: Thickness: 98.259646–350.139815 m; accumulation is zero.

19
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Locate Download results beneath the plot.
Why: The archive keeps this run’s numeric outputs, figures and provenance together.
Look for: Save the current run’s archive.

20
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Choose Download results.
Why: The archive keeps this run’s numeric outputs, figures and provenance together.
Look for: Wait for preparation to finish and a new HTTPS link.

21
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Allow the result archive to finish preparing.
Why: Keep the source, versions, hashes and scientific qualifications with the result.
Look for: The following view resumes after preparation.

22
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Open the new link and save the 19-member ZIP.
Why: The archive keeps this run’s numeric outputs, figures and provenance together.
Look for: Keep fields, mesh, figures and runtime/RNG provenance together.

23
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:09.000
Keep the native numerical context with your result.
Why: Keep the scientific input definitions and numerical qualifications with the result.
